WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:12.100
FedRAMP change management is the controlled process for proposing, reviewing, approving, implementing, validating, documenting, and monitoring changes to a cloud service offering.

2
00:00:12.200 --> 00:00:17.880
It matters because cloud services do not stay still after assessment or authorization support work.

3
00:00:17.980 --> 00:00:29.420
Code is deployed, infrastructure changes, identities are added, vendors evolve, vulnerabilities are remediated, logging pipelines are adjusted, and customer-facing features expand.

4
00:00:29.520 --> 00:00:31.680
Each change can affect risk.

5
00:00:31.960 --> 00:00:36.920
Change management is related to several other disciplines, but it is not the same thing.

6
00:00:37.020 --> 00:00:42.060
Configuration management tracks and maintains expected system states.

7
00:00:42.160 --> 00:00:45.760
Release management coordinates delivery of software or service updates.

8
00:00:45.860 --> 00:00:49.140
Vulnerability remediation fixes known weaknesses.

9
00:00:49.240 --> 00:00:51.800
Incident response handles security events.

10
00:00:51.900 --> 00:00:55.420
Continuous monitoring watches the operating system over time.

11
00:00:55.520 --> 00:01:02.400
Change management provides the governance and evidence path for deciding whether a proposed change is acceptable and proving what happened.

12
00:01:02.680 --> 00:01:10.600
A change can affect security posture, evidence quality, authorization boundaries, inherited controls, and customer trust.

13
00:01:10.700 --> 00:01:13.900
A small network update might expose a service.

14
00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:17.680
A logging change might reduce investigation capability.

15
00:01:17.780 --> 00:01:20.820
A dependency update might alter the system boundary.

16
00:01:20.920 --> 00:01:23.800
An identity change might grant privileged access.

17
00:01:23.900 --> 00:01:27.260
A new vendor integration might shift shared responsibility.

18
00:01:27.360 --> 00:01:34.560
The point is not to fear change; it is to manage change with enough context to understand its security impact.

19
00:01:34.840 --> 00:01:41.240
Strong change management is an operational security discipline, not just a ticketing workflow.

20
00:01:41.340 --> 00:01:52.460
A ticket can record the decision, but the program depends on ownership, impact analysis, approvals, testing, validation, rollback planning, documentation updates, and monitoring.

21
00:01:52.560 --> 00:01:55.440
The goal is not slowing engineering down.

22
00:01:55.540 --> 00:02:02.340
The goal is making changes safe, traceable, reviewable, validated, and aligned with the authorized or FedRAMP-aligned system.
